<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#luxembourgish">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luxembourgish</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luxembourgish is a West Germanic language, derived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> Franconian dialects, spoken as a mother tongue by most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourgers">Luxembourgers</a>. Long regarded as a German dialect, it became a symbol of national identity during and after the German occupation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and a law of 1984 declared it the national language. French and German remain languages of administration, law and the press alongside it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luxembourgish.jpg" alt="Luxembourgish" width="320" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Erren · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moselfrankisch.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
